\begin{abstract}
We propose a fundamental ontological shift in the description of physical reality, moving from the probabilistic fields of 20th-century gauge theories to a constructive geometry of physical four-dimensional worldline filaments termed the \textbf{Zottenwelt} [6, 9]. Within this Scalar-Angular-Torsion (SAT) framework, spacetime is reinterpreted as a discrete 24-cell hypersphere unit cell vertex (HSUCV) lattice [10-12]. We define fundamental particles as \textbf{intersectional traces}—the statistical 3D projections where 4D filaments pierce a propagating time-surface wavefront [7, 8, 13]. This architecture eliminates the need for 19+ manually tuned parameters, establishing a \textbf{Zero-Parameter Economy} anchored by a single filamental scale [8, 14, 15]. We further report that this filamental reconstruction demonstrates a 9.6x efficiency ratio over the Standard Model in producing identical physical observables [16-18].
\end{abstract}

